@page rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · LEAH · STANDING" m="TKC: MEN · DR3; VOL. I · {v1:sorted} · {v1:arrangement} · {v1:household}"
# The clerk's version and the true one
rec:
On the ledger | A Commonwife, two years Bound, with the child quota pressure starting (MEN).
Her husband | A common man in Aurelia's service sector, "not cruel, not kind, simply present," who got a wife because the system said he was eligible and she was available. He expects what the Empire told him he could expect (MEN).
Her two days | She comes to Celeste's twice a week under cover of helping with Solomon. She does help, and it's also the only two days she can breathe. She and Zion sit at the table and don't talk about what they can't talk about (MEN).
The evenings | She watches Eden's score move on the kitchen screen. When the Broadcast narrative shifts in Phase Three, something in her that's been quiet for two years goes very still and then very loud, and she doesn't let anyone in the house see (MEN).
Home Instruction | At her assessment Bell offered to nominate her for Home Instruction the following year, as a consolation (DR3). OPEN QUESTION: What is it?
prompt: What her own house looks like, and what she does there on the other five days | fill

@page rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · LEAH · FAITH AND SCRIPTURE · 1 OF 2" m="TKC: MEN · DR3 · FR 14; VOL. I · {v1:teaches} · {v1:arrangement}"
# What she believes
rec:
What she believes | She believes in Eden completely (MEN). INFERENCE: Her faith is in a person before it's in a doctrine.
Who taught her | Celeste, from the age of six. Before that, Miriam, and Leah remembers her mother's leaving as a decision (FR 14).
What she defends | Miriam, to the others (FR 14). INTERPRETATION: She's the angriest and the most loyal, and those come from the same place.
What she has no words for | Being unready, because the Empire hasn't built a way to say it (MEN).
empire: What the Temple teaches about a woman like her
A Commonwife who doesn't conceive on the Covenant timeline is a household problem with a quarterly visit (MEN). POSSIBLE: The Temple preaches "be fruitful, and multiply" (Gen 1.28) and 1 Timothy 2.15, "she shall be saved in childbearing," and reads Michal's "no child unto the day of her death" (2 Sam 6.23) as a sentence passed on a woman who despised her husband.
/end
prompt: What she prays for, and whether she wants it answered | fill

@page rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · LEAH · FAITH AND SCRIPTURE · 2 OF 2" m="GEN 29–30; GEN 29.32; GEN 30.1; 1 SAM 1; ISA 54.1; ISA 56.3–5; LUKE 11.27–28; PS 113.9"
# The text
rec:
Her name | Leah, Jacob's first wife, given to him by trickery, "tender eyed," and unloved. She bears six sons and a daughter, and names her first son saying "now therefore my husband will love me" (Gen 29.32).
Where it breaks | The biblical Leah is the fertile wife. Leah Osei carries the name of the woman with children and lives the story of the sister without them. Rachel says "Give me children, or else I die" (Gen 30.1).
Hannah · 1 Samuel 1 | POSSIBLE: Provoked for years, praying with her lips moving and no sound, and taken for drunk by the priest. **Where it breaks:** Hannah's husband asks "am not I better to thee than ten sons?" Leah's husband makes the officer's comment, less carefully (MEN).
tab:
| She'd quote | Never read to her in the Temple |
| Genesis 29.32, if she ever let herself say it | Isaiah 54.1, "Sing, O barren, thou that didst not bear" |
| _ | Isaiah 56.4–5, promised to eunuchs who'd have no children: "a name better than of sons and of daughters" |
| _ | Luke 11.27–28, "Blessed is the womb that bare thee… Yea rather, blessed are they that hear the word of God, and keep it" |
small: The Temple likes Psalm 113.9, "He maketh the barren woman to keep house, and to be a joyful mother of children."
box: Volume One
Genesis, the women, 252. Hannah, Abigail and Michal, 380. The Binding, 510. Commonwives and the Covenant timeline, 512. Being sorted, and Leah's assessment, 508.
/end

